WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:07.759
Fire can bring entire buildings to the ground
in hours and can devastate entire forests

00:00:07.759 --> 00:00:10.260
that have stood for thousands of years.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:13.450
It's one of the most powerful forces of nature.

00:00:13.450 --> 00:00:16.510
But what exactly is fire?

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:20.480
When we stare into a flame, what are we actually
looking at?

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:23.910
Let's find out.

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:31.250
The ancient Greeks believed that the universe
was made up of four basic elements, fire,

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:33.740
water, earth and air.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:38.140
Their belief was built on the premise that
you can touch and feel all of these things,

00:00:38.140 --> 00:00:41.200
they believed they were the building blocks
of all matter.

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.260
We now know that this isn't the case.

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:48.300
But the ancient Greeks got one thing in particular,
very, very wrong.

00:00:48.300 --> 00:00:54.160
They completely miss-classified fire, because,
fire isn't an element at all.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:58.190
Also it's not really matter either, well not
completely anyway.

00:00:58.190 --> 00:01:03.700
Fire is a mixture of gases such as oxygen,
carbon dioxide, carbon monoxide and water

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:09.140
vapour, to name a few; mix those gases with
photons of light and thermal energy and you

00:01:09.140 --> 00:01:12.020
pretty much have the building blocks of fire.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:16.700
Fire is actually the visible side effect of
what happens when matter undergoes a change

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:19.680
of state by means of a chemical reaction.

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.250
Of course, you can't blame the Greeks, they
did pretty well considering their level of

00:01:23.250 --> 00:01:25.299
technological advancement.

00:01:25.299 --> 00:01:29.909
But let's see if we can do a litter better
with everything we now know about atoms, elements

00:01:29.909 --> 00:01:31.890
and chemical reactions.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.520
Fire isn't just fire.

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.980
There's no element or chemical substance known
as fire.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:38.280
So what is it?

00:01:38.280 --> 00:01:43.470
Well, fire is the name we have given to a
visual phenomenon that occurs when matter

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:44.470
burns.

00:01:44.470 --> 00:01:49.920
In truth fire is a complex beast and in order
to explain exactly what it is, we need to

00:01:49.920 --> 00:01:52.630
break it down into it core components.

00:01:52.630 --> 00:01:57.320
The first and most important component of
fire is the fuel.

00:01:57.320 --> 00:01:59.829
This is what's actually burning.

00:01:59.829 --> 00:02:03.670
Take a candle for example, the fuel is the
wax.

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:08.039
When we set the wick alight the heat causes
the molecules in the wax, which are mostly

00:02:08.039 --> 00:02:11.950
carbon and hydrogen, to move about erratically.

00:02:11.950 --> 00:02:16.969
This causes the covalent chemical bonds which
are holding the molecules together, the break

00:02:16.969 --> 00:02:18.290
apart.

00:02:18.290 --> 00:02:24.640
Releasing individual hydrogen and carbon atoms,
as well as hydrogen-carbon molecules.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.940
These individual atoms and molecules form
a gas which travels upwards from the candle.

00:02:29.940 --> 00:02:32.370
This process is known as Pyrolysis.

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:35.090
The gas travels upwards because of gravity.

00:02:35.090 --> 00:02:39.189
The air immediately surrounding the candle
flame get's heated up.

00:02:39.189 --> 00:02:44.790
The cold air surrounding the hot puts pressure
on the hot air, because cold air is more dense

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:45.889
than hot air.

00:02:45.889 --> 00:02:51.900
The pressure exerted from the cold air pushes
the hot air upwards and the heat rises, as

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:57.239
this happens the cold air replaces the hot
air and gets heated up itself.

00:02:57.239 --> 00:03:01.400
This process repeats itself for as long as
the flame is alight.

00:03:01.400 --> 00:03:03.949
This is a process known as convection.

00:03:03.949 --> 00:03:09.030
But heat only rises in this fashion in gravity
rich environments.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:14.360
In outer space flames don't travel upwards,
they instead radiate in a perfectly spherical

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:16.379
pattern from the source.

00:03:16.379 --> 00:03:20.519
So now we have established fire's second component,
gas.

00:03:20.519 --> 00:03:25.309
The elements that make up the gas in fire
depends entirely on the fuel.

00:03:25.309 --> 00:03:29.370
If you change the fuel, you also change the
properties of the fire.

00:03:29.370 --> 00:03:35.199
But there's still two more components to fire,
light and heat.

00:03:35.199 --> 00:03:41.419
So far we have deduced that fire is mostly
gas but gases don't usually give off light,

00:03:41.419 --> 00:03:43.729
so where does the light come from.

00:03:43.729 --> 00:03:45.939
This is where oxygen comes in.

00:03:45.939 --> 00:03:51.219
We all know that oxygen, or some other highly
oxygenated substance is required to make a

00:03:51.219 --> 00:03:53.529
fire and keep it burning.

00:03:53.529 --> 00:03:58.280
So back to the candle, when the gas rises
from the candle the atoms in the gas are still

00:03:58.280 --> 00:04:01.510
moving around vigorously because of the heat.

00:04:01.510 --> 00:04:06.669
These excited, but clumsy, atoms collide with
oxygen atoms in the air.

00:04:06.669 --> 00:04:10.609
When the two atoms meet they interact with
one-another to cause a chemical reaction.

00:04:10.609 --> 00:04:16.810
Some of the energy from this reaction gets
released as photons of light and so we see

00:04:16.810 --> 00:04:20.479
a bright blue, orange, red or white flicker.

00:04:20.479 --> 00:04:23.199
This process is known as chemiluminescence.

00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:27.919
The remaining atoms that don't collide with
oxygen get carried up the flame and eventually

00:04:27.919 --> 00:04:34.150
heat up to a level where they give off visible
light, this is known as incandescence.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:39.900
Now imagine billions of these tiny, chemiluminescent
and incandescent chemical reactions, producing

00:04:39.900 --> 00:04:45.629
billions of tiny little flickers of light,
every second - that is what makes a flame

00:04:45.629 --> 00:04:46.849
burn so brightly.

00:04:46.849 --> 00:04:52.090
The colour of the flame depends of the molecules
that make up the fuel and subsequently the

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:54.560
gas which is releases when burned.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:57.960
The temperature at which it burns also effects
the colour.

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:01.979
You can therefore estimate the temperature
of a flame based on its colour.

00:05:01.979 --> 00:05:07.270
A red flame is anywhere from 525 to 1,000
degrees Celsius.

00:05:07.270 --> 00:05:14.289
Orange is between 1,000 and 1,2000 degrees
Celsius and at white flame is around 1,500

00:05:14.289 --> 00:05:15.289
degrees.

00:05:15.289 --> 00:05:21.750
But a blue flame is significantly hotter at
around 3,000 degrees Celsius.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:26.389
And so to the final component of fire, heat.

00:05:26.389 --> 00:05:28.300
Why is fire hot?

00:05:28.300 --> 00:05:29.759
Well this is really quite simple.

00:05:29.759 --> 00:05:33.930
The heat you feel from a flame is just the
energy being released from the billions of

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:39.409
chemical reactions occurring between the atoms
in the flame and the oxygen in the air.

00:05:39.409 --> 00:05:44.770
All chemical reactions either give off energy
as a byproduct or absorb energy.

00:05:44.770 --> 00:05:50.110
The chemical reactions that happen in a fire
are exothermic, which means they give off

00:05:50.110 --> 00:05:56.310
energy; as opposed to endothermic which is
when a reaction absorbs energy.

00:05:56.310 --> 00:06:01.039
So to summarise, what exactly is it you're
seeing when you look into a flame?

00:06:01.039 --> 00:06:06.300
You're seeing a gas undergoing billions of
tiny chemical reactions which are giving off

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:08.409
photons of light and thermal energy.

00:06:08.409 --> 00:06:13.689
Oh and you're also looking at millions of
tiny diamonds being created and disappearing

00:06:13.689 --> 00:06:15.550
right before your eyes.

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:22.139
Yes diamonds, in a flame, the carbon atoms
released from the fuel come together to create

00:06:22.139 --> 00:06:28.779
diamond nanoparticles, millions of times per
second.

